task:
Implement the repository preparation slice of issue #540: a fresh, hardened self-hosted Octopus installation on the new `ed-finder-prod` server, without migrating the legacy Octopus database/Qdrant state. Start from exact current origin/main `fd64ef8e14b5b026cec2d14949115ab681fb0b7d`. Read CLAUDE.md, docs/ROADMAP.md, issue #540, current `config/nginx.conf`, `docker-compose.yml`, the ed-new control-plane workflow/runbooks/tests, and the official `octopusreview/octopus` self-hosting files at release `v1.0.122` (especially `.env.example`, `docker-compose.selfhost.yml`, release instructions, App Manifest self-host flow, model/provider resolution, admin bootstrap and migration instructions). The wrapper will commit and push an isolated branch; do not commit, push, merge, deploy, access either server, use credentials, connect to any database, modify DNS/firewalls/GitHub Apps, start or stop containers, or delete legacy data. No Stage 27, V3 runtime, ratings, CRE/CPE, Journal, product or ED-Finder data behavior changes.

This slice is preparation and validation only. Do not alter the current ED-Finder production compose or nginx route yet. Add an isolated, reviewable deployment package under a clearly named path such as `deploy/octopus-selfhost/`, a bounded operator/preflight script under `scripts/operator/`, an operations runbook, and focused tests. Keep every example secret synthetic/placeholder-only.

Required architecture:
- pin the Octopus application to official release/image `1.0.122`, never `latest`;
- dedicated Compose project and dedicated PostgreSQL 17 + Qdrant volumes, completely separate from all ED-Finder/V3 databases and migration ledgers;
- no host-published PostgreSQL or Qdrant ports;
- Octopus web bound only to `127.0.0.1:43300` by default for the private-install phase, with any future shared-edge network attachment documented but not activated in this slice;
- unique generated PostgreSQL password, `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET`, `OCTOPUS_DATA_KEY` and temporary first-admin password; no demonstration/default password such as `octopus`; generated values must be URL-safe (prefer fixed-length hex/base64url) and written only to a root-owned mode-0600 host env file;
- `OCTOPUS_ADMIN_EMAIL` is operator-supplied or a clearly documented placeholder; the temporary password must never be printed by repository tooling or Actions, and Octopus will force its first change;
- provider and GitHub App credentials are deliberately absent from the deployment bundle and must be configured interactively/through host secrets after private health is proven;
- document that self-hosting removes Octopus SaaS/community quotas but not Anthropic/OpenAI provider billing/rate limits; intended initial defaults are Claude review (`claude-sonnet-4-6`) plus OpenAI `text-embedding-3-large`, with Ollama explicitly deferred;
- bounded Docker JSON logging, restart policies and health checks; sensible but non-disruptive resource limits after verifying the official services;
- persistent volumes and an operator-only PostgreSQL backup/restore procedure. Qdrant is rebuildable from repository indexing, but document whether it is backed up or intentionally reconstructed;
- no public edge, TLS, DNS or webhook cutover until a later separately authorised slice.

Preflight/prepare tooling:
- provide a read-only preflight mode that emits no environment values and checks host identity expectation (`ed-finder-prod`), kernel/architecture, CPU/RAM/disk, Docker/Compose versions, current listeners on 43300/43332/43333/43334 and 80/443, Docker networks, whether `/opt/octopus` exists, and enough public-edge container metadata (names, network names and bind mounts only) to plan a later route; do not inspect container environment variables or database contents;
- provide a preparation mode that creates the deployment directory in a caller-supplied temporary root for tests, generates secrets atomically with restrictive umask, renders/installs the pinned compose/env files, and fails closed on existing non-managed content; production `/opt/octopus` must require an explicit confirmation phrase and root privileges;
- preparation must not start containers, apply migrations or modify nginx/DNS;
- provide a validation mode that runs `docker compose config` when Docker is available and statically verifies pins, listener bindings, volumes, networks and absence of public DB/Qdrant publication;
- design a later install sequence in the runbook: pull, start private dependencies, apply matching-tag Prisma migrations (choose and document a reproducible method that does not depend on an unpinned host toolchain), start web, verify `/api/health` and `/api/version`, restart/persistence test, then interactive admin/provider/GitHub App setup;
- document new-GitHub-App versus reusing-old-App tradeoffs and recommend one. Avoid two simultaneous active webhook paths and require one successful exact-head review before retiring the old instance.

Security/operational contracts:
- no secrets in argv where practical, source, logs, test fixtures, Compose defaults or generated receipts;
- no source of arbitrary shell code from `.env`; parse/render deliberately;
- no curl-pipe-shell installers;
- migration toolchain/version must be pinned to the Octopus tag or image;
- scripts must use `set -euo pipefail` or equivalent, atomic writes, restrictive permissions and clear rollback/cleanup;
- old instance deletion is not implemented in this slice; runbook requires disabling the legacy GitHub webhook/App, stopping it, observing a rollback window, then explicit separate destruction and credential revocation;
- explicitly preserve all existing ED-Finder/V3 containers and ports.

Tests:
- static Compose parse and invariants: exact app tag, no `latest`, no default/demo password, web loopback-only, no host DB/Qdrant ports, dedicated volumes/network, bounded logging/health/restart;
- generated secret shape/uniqueness and file mode without exposing values;
- preflight output contains no env/secrets and does not mutate a synthetic host tree;
- preparation fails closed on unmanaged existing content, requires confirmation/root for `/opt/octopus`, and performs zero Docker start/migration commands;
- migration/install runbook pins the tag/toolchain and requires private health/version proof before edge/GitHub App cutover;
- separation contract forbids ED-Finder database/container names and current app Compose modification;
- documentation records fresh-install data loss, provider-billing truth, exact cutover proof, rollback window and user-interactive steps.

Run all focused tests, relevant operator/workflow/repo-hygiene and CI-stall contracts, Ruff/format for changed Python, shellcheck or `bash -n` for changed shell, parse all YAML with pinned PyYAML/Compose where available, `git diff --check`, and inspect the final diff against main. End with exact files, exact results, chosen migration method, recommended GitHub App strategy, and limitations: no server write, provider key, app creation, review, DNS/edge cutover or legacy destruction was performed.
